values ('d6e7f8a9-0b1c-4d56-9e56-6f7a8b9c0d86', 'introduction-to-international-relations', 'Introduction to International Relations', 'Students learn what IR studies, how to ask causal questions about world politics, and how individual, state, domestic, and system-level explanations differ. Core goals cover power, anarchy, and the security dilemma; cooperation through institutions, domestic politics, trade, and repeated interaction; ideas, identities, norms, and legitimacy; critical approaches to inequality, hierarchy, gender, empire, capitalism, and silenced voices; war, deterrence, alliances, and security; foreign policy decision-making; treaties, sovereignty, and international organizations; trade, finance, development, and sanctions; global governance; human rights, humanitarian intervention, and accountability; corporations, NGOs, and other nonstate actors; climate, migration, health, and technology; and regional dynamics, great-power competition, and changing world order.');

